# Technology Stack

## Overview

Our primary programming language is TypeScript using Effect, running on NodeJS 22.x runtime.

All our repositories should be using the following tooling to ensure consistency and quality:

- Programming Language: Node 22.x (LTS) + latest TypeScript + latest [Effect](https://effect.website/docs)
- Package Manager: [pnpm](https://pnpm.io/) with extensive use of workspaces installed using corepack
- Linting: [Biome](https://biomejs.dev/)
- Testing: [vitest](https://vitest.dev/)
  - Unit tests: [@effect/vitest](https://effect.website/docs/testing/vitest)
  - E2E tests: [supertest](https://github.com/visionmedia/supertest)
- Infrastructure: AWS preferring cloud native solutions (Lambda, S3, DynamoDB, etc.)
- IoC: [AWS CDK](https://aws.amazon.com/cdk/)
- CI/CD: [GitLab CI/CD](https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/)

## Programming Language

Our primary programming language is TypeScript using Effect, running on NodeJS 22.x runtime.

- Install local node version using [nvm](https://github.com/nvm-sh/nvm)
- Run ts files using `pnpm exec tsx`
- Bundle code using [esbuild](https://esbuild.github.io/) or in case of Lambda functions using The [NodeJSFunction construct](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cdk/api/v2/docs/aws-cdk-lib.aws_lambda_nodejs.NodejsFunction.html)
- Bundle npm packages using [tsup](https://tsup.egoist.dev/)

## Package Manager

Always use pnpm installed via corepack as our package manager.

For all repositories we use pnpm workspaces, which can be initialized by creating a `pnpm-workspace.yaml` file in the root of the repository. once it's initialised using `pnpm init` we can start adding packages to the workspace using `pnpm add <package> --workspace`

An example of a `pnpm-workspace.yaml` file:

```yaml
packages:
  - packages/*
```

## Linting and Formatting

We use Biome for linting and formatting, with the following configuration:

```json
{
 "$schema": "https://biomejs.dev/schemas/2.1.2/schema.json",
 "vcs": {
  "enabled": true,
  "clientKind": "git",
  "useIgnoreFile": true
 },
 "files": {
  "ignoreUnknown": false,
  "includes": [
   "*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx,json}"
  ]
 },
 "formatter": {
  "enabled": true,
  "indentStyle": "tab"
 },
 "organizeImports": {
  "enabled": true
 },
 "linter": {
  "enabled": true,
  "rules": {
   "recommended": true,
   "style": {
    "noParameterAssign": "error",
    "useAsConstAssertion": "error",
    "useDefaultParameterLast": "error",
    "useEnumInitializers": "error",
    "useSelfClosingElements": "error",
    "useSingleVarDeclarator": "error",
    "noUnusedTemplateLiteral": "error",
    "useNumberNamespace": "error",
    "noInferrableTypes": "error",
    "noUselessElse": "error"
   }
  }
 },
 "javascript": {
  "formatter": {
   "quoteStyle": "single",
   "semicolons": "asNeeded"
  }
 }
}
```

## Testing

Use [vitest](https://vitest.dev/) as a test runner,

- Unit tests: [@effect/vitest](https://effect.website/docs/testing/vitest)
- E2E tests: [supertest](https://github.com/visionmedia/supertest)

Examples:

```ts
// Unit test example
import { describe, it, expect } from "@effect/vitest";
import { Effect } from "effect";

describe("my effect", () => {
  it.effect("succeeds", () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const result = yield* Effect.succeed(42);
      expect(result).toBe(42);
    })
  );
});

// E2E test example
import { describe, it, expect } from "@effect/vitest";
import request from "supertest";

describe("Foo API", () => {
  it("should return 200", () =>
    request("https://some-api.com").get("/foo").expect(200)
  );
});
```

## Infrastructure

We use [AWS CDK](https://aws.amazon.com/cdk/) to define our infrastructure.

## CI/CD

We use [GitLab CI/CD](https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/) to run our tests and deploy our code.

The flow typically follows the following stages:

- Prepare dependencies and cache the pnpm cache
- Run code quality checks:
  - Linting
  - Unit tests
- Deploy the code to the non-prod environment(s)
  - On topic branches there is only a manual step to deploy to `mx-play`
  - On the default branch it automatically deploys to both `mx-play` and `staging`
- Run E2E tests against the `staging` environment
- Deploy the code to the `production` environment (if fully tested this is automatically triggered, otherwise it's a manual step)
- Deploy the updated documentation to Gitlab Pages

## Git tooling

The agreed convention for commit messages is [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/).

To catch issues early we use the following git tooling, where linting, formatting and unit tests are run on the pre-commit hook.

- [Husky](https://typicode.github.io/husky/#/) for pre-commit hooks
- [lint-staged](https://github.com/okonet/lint-staged) for running linting and formatting on staged files
- [commitlint](https://commitlint.js.org/) for commit message validation
- [commitizen](https://commitizen.github.io/cz-cli/) for interactive commit messages

## Documentation

We use [GitLab Pages](https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/user/project/pages/) to deploy our documentation.

The documentation is written in [Markdown](https://www.markdownguide.org/) and is deployed to Gitlab Pages.
